arm64: dts: mediatek: mt6797: give the A72 cluster a clock

cpu8/cpu9 get clocks and clock-names, naming the mux clk/0003 adds and the
same armpll_div_pll1 the eight A53s park on. With proc-supply (dts/0060) and
an OPP table (dts/0062) that is everything mediatek-cpufreq asks for, and
"mtk-cpufreq: cpu8: no operating-points-v2, not managing it" stops being the
end of the story.

The capacity comment is rewritten but the NUMBERS ARE UNCHANGED, and that is
the point. An earlier draft of this patch restated them as work per MHz here,
which is right for a board that renormalises and wrong for every board that
does not: the kernel only multiplies capacity-dmips-mhz by cpuinfo_max_freq
once every possible CPU has a cpufreq policy, and mt6797-evb and
mt6797-x20-dev include this dtsi with no OPP tables anywhere, so on those two
the raw values are final. Restating them here would have shipped a silent
quarter-weight change to boards this work never touches, straight into the
upstreaming path. The per-MHz restatement belongs with the OPP tables that
make it necessary, and it is in mt6797-gemini-pda.dts (dts/0062).

What the comment now says instead is which of the two situations a reader is
in, and where the other one is handled.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797.dtsi
@@ -27,15 +27,28 @@
 		#size-cells = <0>;
 
 		/*
-		 * Measured single-thread throughput at each cluster's ceiling
-		 * (gbench stint, Gemini PDA 2026-08-24): A53-LL 642 Mops/s at
-		 * 1222 MHz, A53-L 689 at 1209, A72 1143 at 2002. Encoded as
-		 * the at-ceiling ratio because the big cluster has no cpufreq
-		 * policy, so the kernel's frequency renormalisation never runs
-		 * and these raw values become the scheduler capacities as-is.
-		 * They are what makes asym_cpu_capacity_scan() see a
-		 * big.LITTLE machine at boot — set later (e.g. from a driver),
-		 * the scan has already run symmetric and never reruns.
+		 * Measured single-thread throughput per cluster, normalised to the
+		 * A72 (gbench stint with the frequency pinned, Gemini PDA
+		 * 2026-08-28): A53-LL 676 Mops/s at 1222 MHz, A53-L 686 at 1209,
+		 * A72 1135 at 1989 -> 608 : 617 : 1024.
+		 *
+		 * These are the values a board with no cpufreq gets, and they are
+		 * final for such a board: the kernel only renormalises
+		 * capacity-dmips-mhz by cpuinfo_max_freq once EVERY possible CPU has
+		 * a cpufreq policy (init_cpu_capacity_callback() waits for
+		 * cpus_to_visit to empty), and with no OPP tables here that never
+		 * happens. mt6797-evb and mt6797-x20-dev are both in that position.
+		 *
+		 * A board that DOES describe its OPPs makes renormalisation run, and
+		 * then these raw numbers are the wrong ones -- so such a board has to
+		 * restate them per MHz. mt6797-gemini-pda.dts does exactly that, and
+		 * carries the arithmetic.
+		 *
+		 * Either way they must be in the device tree: asym_cpu_capacity_scan()
+		 * runs at sched_init_domains() and again only when
+		 * arch_update_cpu_topology() reports a change, which plain hotplug
+		 * never does -- capacities set from an initcall are invisible to the
+		 * asymmetry machinery for ever (measured, builds #66-#67).
 		 */
 		cpu0: cpu@0 {
 			device_type = "cpu";
@@ -130,6 +143,9 @@
 			compatible = "arm,cortex-a72";
 			enable-method = "psci";
 			reg = <0x200>;
+			clocks = <&mcumixed CLK_MCU_B_SEL>,
+				 <&topckgen CLK_TOP_ARMPLL_DIV_PLL1>;
+			clock-names = "cpu", "intermediate";
 			capacity-dmips-mhz = <1024>;
 		};
 
@@ -138,6 +154,9 @@
 			compatible = "arm,cortex-a72";
 			enable-method = "psci";
 			reg = <0x201>;
+			clocks = <&mcumixed CLK_MCU_B_SEL>,
+				 <&topckgen CLK_TOP_ARMPLL_DIV_PLL1>;
+			clock-names = "cpu", "intermediate";
 			capacity-dmips-mhz = <1024>;
 		};
 
